# Rules for User Service Security
## 1. Authentication Rules
- All API endpoints must require JWT authentication except explicitly public endpoints
- JWT tokens must expire after 24 hours
- Refresh tokens must be used for extending sessions and should expire after 7 days
- Invalid login attempts must be limited to 5 tries before triggering a 15-minute lockout
- Passwords must be hashed using bcrypt with minimum cost factor of 10
- Store only hashed passwords, never plaintext

## 2. Authorization Rules
- Use role-based access control (RBAC) for all endpoints
- Admin role: Full access to all endpoints
- Teacher role: Access to course management, exam creation, and student data
- Student role: Access to own data, courses, and public content
- Guest role: Access only to public endpoints
- Always verify resource ownership before allowing CRUD operations

## 3. API Rate Limiting
- Apply global rate limiting: 100 requests per minute per IP
- Apply specific rate limiting for sensitive operations:
  - Login/Registration: 5 attempts per 15 minutes
  - Password reset: 3 attempts per 30 minutes
  - API key generation: 3 attempts per hour

## 4. Data Validation
- Validate all input data with appropriate schema validation
- Sanitize all user inputs to prevent injection attacks
- Enforce strong password policy:
  - Minimum 8 characters
  - Require uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and special characters
  - Reject common passwords and sequential characters
- Validate email addresses with proper format checking and domain verification

## 5. Session Management
- Allow only one active session per user (optional config)
- Invalidate all sessions when a user changes password
- Track and log all session activities for audit purposes

## 6. Security Headers
- Set appropriate security headers:
  - Content-Security-Policy
  - X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  - X-Frame-Options: DENY
  - Strict-Transport-Security
  - X-XSS-Protection

## 7. Error Handling
- Never expose sensitive information in error messages
- Log all errors with appropriate context but without sensitive data
- Return generic error messages to clients with unique error codes for tracking

## 8. File Upload Rules
- Restrict file types to safe formats (PDF, JPG, PNG, etc.)
- Scan all uploads for malware
- Limit file sizes based on type (e.g., 5MB for images, 20MB for documents)
- Store files outside the web root with randomized names
- Process and validate all image uploads

## 9. API Documentation and Versioning
- Version all APIs (e.g., /v1/users)
- Document all endpoints with OpenAPI/Swagger
- Clearly specify required permissions for each endpoint
- Implement API deprecation policy with proper notifications

## 10. Logging and Monitoring
- Log all authentication attempts (success/failure)
- Monitor and alert on suspicious activity patterns
- Implement audit logging for all sensitive operations
- Retain logs for minimum 90 days
- Never log sensitive information (passwords, tokens, etc.)

## 11. Two-Factor Authentication
- Support 2FA for all user accounts
- Require 2FA for admin accounts and sensitive operations
- Implement backup codes for account recovery
- Set a maximum lifetime for 2FA tokens (5 minutes)

## 12. Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)
- Restrict allowed origins to specific domains
- Implement proper OPTIONS handling
- Allow only necessary HTTP methods per endpoint
- Restrict allowed headers and credentials
